$DIR/main.p:22:10: cannot use c.Close as a value: Close has a Conn receiver, so it consumes c
    Conn is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it when
    they are dropped. A method with a value receiver of an owner type consumes
    its receiver: a call moves the receiver into the method. A method value
    would hold c, and every call of it would consume c again, so a consuming
    method has none.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Call the method where it is needed:
        c.Close()
    Or use the method expression, which takes the receiver as its first
    argument:
        f := Conn.Close
        f(c)
    (see "Method values" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.9)
    (run "pluto explain E3315" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:26:10: cannot use p.Close as a value: Close has a Conn receiver, so it consumes p.Conn
    Conn is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it when
    they are dropped. A method with a value receiver of an owner type consumes
    its receiver: a call moves the receiver into the method. A method value
    would hold p.Conn, and every call of it would consume p.Conn again, so a
    consuming method has none.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Call the method where it is needed:
        take(&p.Conn).Close()
    Or use the method expression, which takes the receiver as its first
    argument:
        f := Conn.Close
        f(take(&p.Conn))
    (see "Method values" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.9)
    (run "pluto explain E3315" for a longer explanation)
